  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 134 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nIt's been quite breezy this morning across portions of the plains \nand increased flow aloft and weak subsidence has allowed gusts up to \n25-40 mph. Guidance is in good agreement that these breezy winds \nwill stick around this afternoon, before a noticeable drop-off \noccurs around sunset. We should stay mostly dry today as current \nwater vapor satellite imagery shows drier air being advected into \nthe region with the upper level low tracking across Idaho and \nMontana. However, we still could see a few showers (15-20% chance)\nand possibly a thunderstorm later this evening across Lincoln \ncounty. Some hi-res guidance seems a bit bullish with the \npotential, but with SPC mesoanalysis showing weak instability \nlater today, can't rule out gusty outflows with any thunderstorm \nthat does develop. \n\nA pattern change is still looking likely on Saturday as a strong \ncold front is slated to arrive early in the morning. This front \nwill bring gusty northerly winds (potentially up to 30-40 mph at \ntimes across the northern plains) and much anticipated cooler \ntemperatures. For highs on Saturday, we should only reach up to \nthe mid-50s across the plains. Aloft, the upper level shortwave in\nsouthern California will become a closed low, with southwest flow\nadvecting moisture into the region once again. This will bring \nanother round of rain showers (and high mountain snow) from the \nsouth to the north by the afternoon and evening. As of right now, \nCAMs favor the bulk of the precipitation with the eastern plains, \nas the Denver metro and areas north will be battling the dry \nnorthern air. However, short-range forecast soundings do show some\nmoistening of the lower levels which could allow a few showers \nbringing light rainfall there by the evening. \n\nGuidance has come to slightly better agreement for Sunday as the \nupper level low weakens and treks across Colorado throughout the \nday. Recent GEFS run has noticeably trended warmer for \ntemperatures (aligning more with the ECMWF) as the drier solutions\nlook more favorable for the morning and early afternoon. For this\nreason, did lean on the NBM and nudge max temperatures up a \ncouple of degrees, and won't be surprised if some portions of the \nplains reach the low 60s. Despite the warmer/drier solutions \nlooking favorable, we still could see rain/snow showers (30-40% \nchance) in the mountains Sunday morning. As the upper level system\nbegins exiting Colorado late Sunday into early Monday morning, \nthat is when the plains have better chance for an additional round\nof rain showers.\n\nWe will start to dry out by late Monday morning as weak \nsubsidence takes over, with relatively weak flow aloft. By \nTuesday/Wednesday, flow aloft will increase and turn more \nwesterly/southwesterly ahead of another upper level shortwave. \nSome uncertainty remains with the track of the system, but the \nshortwave may be too far north of Colorado for us to see decent \nprecipitation. With cross-barrier flow increasing, we could be \nlooking at breezy-to-strong winds for our high county. Too early \nto be sure, but worth watching out for. In terms of temperatures, \nensemble guidance does favor slightly above seasonal normals (mid \n60s) through the end of the week.",
